  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Human Trafficking in Medieval Europe (2025), published by Taylor & Francis, has been cited 10 times \u2014 enough that OpenAlex puts it in the top 10% of works in its field and year. Beyond that, it has been downloaded 3,766 times and is discussed on Wikipedia.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Human Trafficking in Medieval Europe by Christopher Paolella was published by Taylor & Francis in 2025. It is part of the MODID-d66f183fd58:Amsterdam University Press series. Conceptually, the work connects to Human trafficking, Entitlement (fair division), Vulnerability (computing), Criminology and Poverty.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">OpenAlex records 10 citations. That places it in the top 10% of works in its field and year of publication. Citations peaked in 2021 with 3 citing works. Notable citing venues include De Gruyter eBooks, Trends in Organized Crime and Historische Zeitschrift. The citing works include 6 journal articles and 1 book chapter. They come from 4 countries, led by the United States, Germany and the United Kingdom.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">It is discussed on Wikipedia (1 mention). It has been downloaded 3,766 times via OAPEN. A further 1 mention appear in other attention signals. Its subject matter aligns with Gender equality among the Sustainable Development Goals.</p>",
